window.PYQ_OCR['telegram'].push({t:'1239140977_47026_Edzorb_Qbank.pdf',q:[{"n": 1, "q": "At the Union level, no demand for a grant can be made except on the recommendation of the President of India Which of the statements given above is/are correct? a) 1 only b) 2 only c) Both 1 and 2 d) Neither 1 nor 2 Ans: B Exp: Union Budget • The Annual Financial Statement (Union Budget) is mandated under Article 112 of the Constitution of India) • It is prepared by the Government of India but is caused to be laid before Parliament by the President of India) • The Finance -Minister presents the Union Budget in Parliament, but this is done on behalf of the President, not the Prime Minister. • The Union Budget consists of Revenue Budget (receipts and expenditure) and Capital Budget (capital receipts and expenditure). • No demand for a grant can be made in the Lok Sabha except on the recommendation of the President of India (Article 113). • The Lok Sabha alone has the power to vote on demands for grants; the Rajya Sabha cannot vote but may discuss the Budget. 1 2 3 4 Q.2) Who of the following is the author of the books “The India Way” and “Why Bharat Matters”? a) Bhupendra Yadav b) Nalin Mehta c) Shashi Tharoor d) Subrahmanyam Jaishankar Ans: D Exp: Subrahmanyam Jaishankar’s Works Book Explanation The India Way • Year of Publication: 2020 • Theme: India’s foreign policy strategies in a multipolar world; balancing relations with US, China, Russia, Europe • Significance: Highlights the India’s pursuit of strategic autonomy and pragmatic diplomacy in global affairs Why Bharat Matters • Year of Publication: 2024 • Theme: The India’s civilizational identity, cultural heritage, and global role in shaping the future • Significance: Stresses importance of viewing India as Bharat, linking diplomacy with civilizational ethos Q.3) Consider the following statements: Statement-I: If the United States of America (USA) were to default on its debt, holders of US Treasury Bonds will not be able to exercise their claims to receive payment. Statement-II: The USA Government debt is not backed by any hard assets, but only by the faith of the Government. Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements? a) Both Statement -I and Statement -II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I b) Both Statement -I and Statement -II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I c) Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect d) Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct Ans: D Exp: The USA Government Debt • Statement‑I is incorrect because even if the United States of America were to default on its debt obligations, holders of US Treasury Bonds legally retain their claims to receive payment. • A default does not extinguish the contractual right of bondholders; it only means that the government has failed to meet its obligations on time. 01 02 • Statement‑II is correct because US Government debt is not backed by tangible or hard assets like gold or lanD) Instead, it is backed solely by the “full faith and credit ” of the US Government. • This phrase signifies the government’s authority to levy taxes and its credibility in international financial markets. • Investors trust the US Government ’s ability and willingness to honour its debt, which makes US Treasury securities one of the safest investments globally despite the absence of asset backing. 03 04 05 Q.4) Consider the following statements: Statement-I: Syndicated lending spreads the risk of borrower default across multiple lenders. Statement-II: The syndicated loan can be a fixed amount/lump sum of funds, but cannot be a credit line. Which one of the f ollowing is correct in respect of the above statements?", "o": ["Both Statement -I and Statement -II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement -I and Statement -II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct Ans: C Exp: Syndicated Loan • Syndicated lending is a process where a group of banks or financial institutions jointly provide funds to a single borrower under a common loan agreement. • The purpose of syndication is to spread the risk of borrower default across multiple lenders, so that no single lender bears the entire risk. • Syndicated loans are typically used for large-scale financing needs, such as infrastructure projects, the corporate expansions, or government borrowings • A syndicated loan can take different forms: Term Loan , and Revolving Credit Facility or Credit Line. 1 2 3 4 • Statement-II is incorrect because syndicated loans are not limited to lump sum disbursements; they can also be structured as credit lines. • The correct interpretation is that syndicated loans are flexible in structure, and the form depends on the borrower’s requirements and the agreement among participating lenders. • Therefore, StatementI is correct because the syndicated lending indeed spr eads risk among lenders. Q.5) Consider the following statements in respect of the digital rupee:"]}, {"n": 2, "q": "It is freely convertible against commercial bank money and cash. Which of the statements given above are correct?", "o": ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 Ans: D Exp: The Digital Rupee • The digital rupee is a sovereign currency issued by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), and it is fully aligned with the RBI’s monetary policy. • Being a form of central bank money, the digital rupee appears as a liability on the RBI ’s balance sheet, just like physical currency notes. • The digital rupee is not insured against inflation by its design; like physical currency, it is subject to inflationary pressures depending on the macroeconomic conditions. • The digital rupee is freely convertible against commercial bank money and cash, ensuring seamless use in transactions and parity with existing forms of money. 1 2 3 4"]}]});
window.PYQ_OCR['telegram'].push({t:'1239140977_47361_05_October_2026.pdf',q:[{"n": 1, "q": "The Kakatiya Rudreshwara (Ramappa) Temple, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is located in which district?", "o": ["Warangal Urban", "Mulugu", "Bhadradri Kothagudem", "Jayashankar Bhupalpally"]}, {"n": 2, "q": "Under Rule 25 of the Registration of Electors Rules, 1960, a revision of electoral rolls under Section 21(2) of the RP Act, 1950 may be:", "o": ["Only intensive", "Only special", "Intensive, summary, or partly intensive and partly summary", "Only summary, unless Parliament directs otherwise"]}, {"n": 3, "q": "In which case did the Supreme Court hold that a foreigner’s fundamental rights are conﬁned to Article 21?", "o": ["Hans Muller of Nurenburg (1955)", "Louis De Raedt v. Union of India (1991)", "Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978)", "NHRC v. State of Arunachal Pradesh (1996)"]}, {"n": 4, "q": "Which surveillance system reported 50 rabies deaths in 2023?", "o": ["MCCD of the Registrar-General", "ICMR-NIE community study", "IDSP-IHIP of the Health Ministry", "WHO Global Health Observatory"]}, {"n": 5, "q": "Green Energy Corridor Phase III aims to evacuate up to how much renewable energy?", "o": ["50 GW", "100 GW", "135 GW", "400 GW QUIZ · MAP Map drill The Godavari-Krishna country around the Ramappa Temple (cutting 1), names removed. Name each numbered site; one pin is a decoy that is not a Kakatiya or Godavari-basin site. Check the key at the back. It is okay if you cannot place all of these today. You will see a map here most days - with time, they stick. Attempt, check, move on. REVISION Quick facts Everything from today worth carrying forward, and nothing else. A few minutes on the day’s ﬁle. POLITY AND GOVERNANCE RP Act, 1950: Section 21(2) intensive or summary revision (Rule 25, 1960 Rules); 21(3) special revision for a constituency or part. ECI in the Constituent Assembly: Draft Article 289. Article 326: adult suﬀrage. CEC Act 2023, Section 16: lifelong immunity for CEC and ECs. Visa ruling: Justice Amit Mahajan, Delhi HC; Immigration and Foreigners Act, 2025 (Sections 3 and 7); Hans Muller (1955), Louis De Raedt (1991). CAPF: SC judgment 23 May 2025; NFFU upheld by Delhi HC 2015, SC 2019; new Act: 50% IG, 67% ADG by deputation. EDUCATION AND SOCIETY VBSA Bill, 2025: an attempt to overhaul higher-education regulation (UGC, NAAC among today’s regulators); UGC 2018 Regulation: 40-hour week. Three-language row: SC extends internal-assessment relief to Class 6; NEP 2020 favours two Indian languages. ECONOMY GST: rate changes from 22 Sep 2025; gross collections AprSep 2026 ₹12.46 lakh crore (+11.6%); refunds ₹1.80 lakh crore; registrations ~1.71 crore; Council meets 7 Oct. Repo hike expected: 25 bps to 5.50%; CPI 4.82% (Aug); US Fed at 3.75-4%. India 10-yr G-sec 7.21%; US public debt >$40 trillion. India-EFTA TEPA: $100 bn investment goal; Switzerland-India Treaty of Friendship turns 80 in 2028. Maharashtra: drought in 265 of 358 talukas; irrigation only"]}, {"n": 6, "q": "18% of cropped area; debt ₹11 lakh crore. ENERGY, ENVIRONMENT AND SCIENCE GEC-III: ₹1.86 lakh crore, 135 GW, 50 GWh BESS, central support ₹54,082 crore, completion FY33; GEC-I due March 2027. Ramappa: mine 5 km downstream, 300 m deep; temple ~800 years old, sand-box foundation. Rabies: IDSP-IHIP 50 (2023), MCCD 271, ICMR-NIE 5,726/yr; India ~35% of global deaths; ~9.1 million animal bites a year. Urban wind: terrestrial stilling since the 1980s; Venturi and downdraught eﬀects; CFD cost scales with the Reynolds number. Loudest birds: bare-throated bellbird and red-legged seriema, 121-122 dB. CITES in force 1 July 1975. CHECK YOURSELF Answers PRELIMS QUIZ 1", "o": ["2", "3", "4", "5"]}]});
