window.PYQ_OCR['telegram'].push({t:'1391868139_7875_June_2026_-_Sunya_Notes_-_Monthly_CA.pdf',q:[{"n": 1, "q": "• Major FDI Destinations (FY 2024 –25): Maharashtra (39% of total FDI equity inflows) > Karnataka (13%) > Delhi (12%) • Major Source Countries (FY 2024 –25): Singapore (30%) > Mauritius (17%) > United States (11%) • Highest FDI Inflows (FY 2024–25): Services sector (19% of total inflows) > Computer software and hardware (16%) > trading (8%) • Cumulative FDI Equity Inflows (2000–2024): ○ Source Countries: Mauritius > Singapore > USA > Netherlands > Japan ○ Sectors: Services Sector, Computer Software & Hardware, Trading, Telecommunications, & Automobile Industry ○ No. of Source Countries : Increased from 89 (FY 2013–14) to 112 (FY 2024–25) • Prohibited Sectors in India: Lottery Business, Chit Funds, Trading in Transferable Development Rights (TDR), Manufacturing of cigarettes (tobacco or tobacco substitutes), Gambling and betting, Nidhi Company, Real Estate Business or Construction of Farmhouses, Sectors not open to priva te sector investments (atomic energy, railway operations) • Recent Development: ○ RBI Framework (2024): Reserve Bank of India introduced a new operational framework for reclassification of Foreign Portfolio Investment (FPI) into FDI. ○ Regulatory Basis: Reclassification is governed by the Foreign Exchange Management (Mode of Payment and Reporting of Non-Debt Instruments) Regulations, 2019. • Significance: FDI brings capital, technology, managerial expertise, employment opportunities, and economic growth, & strengthens India's integration with the global economy. Foreign Currency Non-Resident (Bank) [FCNR", "o": ["] CONTEXT: Recently, RBI introduced a special dispensation allowing commercial banks to mobilize fresh 3 to 5 -year FCNR", ". ● Concept: Fixed-term foreign currency deposit accounts maintained in India by NRIs, OCIs, and PIOs | Unlike regular NRI accounts, deposits are retained in foreign currency and are not converted into Indian Rupees. ● Aim: To provide Indian banks with a stable source of overseas funds while allowing depositors to earn returns without bearing rupee depreciation risk. ● Eligible Currencies: US Dollar (USD), Pound Sterling (GBP), Euro (EUR), Japanese Yen (JPY), Australian Dollar (AUD), & Canadian Dollar (CAD). ● Features: o Deposits are held in foreign currency for a fixed tenure. o Interest is paid in the same foreign currency. o Interest income is tax -free in India as long as the depositor retains non-resident status. o Fresh FCNR", "deposits under the RBI window are exempt from Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) and Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR) requirements. o Interest rates are linked to international benchmarks (generally lower than domestic rupee fixed deposit rates) o Forms part of India's overall NRI deposit base along with NRE and NRO accounts. ● Benefits: ○ Protects NRIs from exchange rate fluctuations and rupee depreciation. ○ Earns interest in foreign currency. ○ Helps NRIs maintain foreign currency savings while banking in India. ○ Strengthens foreign exchange liquidity MONTHLY PRELIMS CURRENT AFFAIRS | JUNE 2026 Subscriber’s Copy | www.sunyaias.com | Prelims Current Affairs 2027 | Telegram: sunyanotes50 Page 60 ● Please Note : Standard Non -Resident External (NRE) or Non-Resident Ordinary (NRO) accounts are maintained in Indian rupees. RBI’s Special Swap Facility (2026): ● Banks can mobilize fresh 3–5 year FCNR", "deposits. ● Banks sell the foreign currency received from depositors to RBI at the prevailing FBIL (Financial Benchmarks India Private Limited) reference rate. ● RBI agrees to sell back the same amount of foreign currency at maturity. ● Buyback is done at par (same exchange rate), with RBI absorbing the forward premium, thereby reducing banks’ hedging costs. Fully Accessible Route (FAR) ● What?: FAR is a special channel through which foreign investors can invest in specified Government of India dated securities (G -Secs) without any investment limit or quantitative caps ● Introduction: By Reserve Bank of India in 2020 ● Objective: To liberalize India’s debt market, improve domestic bond liquidity, and attract stable, long -term international capital. ● Eligible Investors: Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPIs), NonResident Indians (NRIs), and Overseas Citizens of India (OCIs) can freely buy and sell designated FAR bonds. ● Features: ○ Under FAR, FPIs, NRIs, OCIs and other eligible entities can invest in these government securities without quantitative caps. ○ FAR bonds offer free buy–sell access ○ Under the normal FPI route, foreign investors face limits on how much they can invest in Indian government securities. ● Significance: ○ Important for making India’s debt market more competitive and internationally visible. ● Other Routes for Foreign Investment in Indian Debt: ○ General Route (GR): Standard route FPIs use to invest in corporate bonds + It is subject to quantitative limits, unlike FAR. ○ Voluntary Retention Route (VRR): It is a special RBI framework that allows FPIs to invest in Indian debt with fewer regulatory restrictions, provided they voluntarily commit to retain a minimum share of their investments in India for a fixed period. Electronic Gold Receipt (EGR) CONTEXT: Recently, the National Stock Exchange (NSE) of India launched EGRs as a new trading segment. ● What?: Dematerialised securities representing ownership of physical gold stored in SEBI -regulated vaults | Held in demat accounts similar to shares and Exchange Traded Funds (ETFs). ● Mechanism: Physical gold is deposited in accredited vaults → Gold is converted into EGR units → EGR units are credited to investors' demat accounts ● Features: ○ EGRs can be traded on stock exchanges ○ Investors can reconvert EGRs into physical gold ○ Participants: jewellers, refiners, traders, retail investors and institutional investors ● Vault Manager: SEBI-registered entity responsible for providing vaulting services for gold deposited for EGR creation. ● Please Note: Bombay Stock Exchange was the first stock exchange in India to launch EGRs in 2022. Coal Exchange CONTEXT: Recently, the Ministry of Coal notified the Coal Exchange Rules, 2026 ● What?: An electronic commodity-trading platform where buyers and sellers can trade and enter into contracts for physical coal through an online system. ● Aim: Improve transparency, competition and efficient price discovery in coal trading. ● Basis: Introduced following the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Amendment Act, 2025 | This Act provided for the concept of a Mineral Exchange. ● Regulator: Coal Controller Organisation (CCO) is the designated authority for registration, regulation and audit of coal exchanges. ● Eligibility for Setting Up a Coal Exchange: ○ Company registered under the Companies Act, 2013 ○ Must have a demutualised structure (ownership and management separated from trading rights) ○ Minimum net worth of ₹50 crore ● Features of Coal Exchange Rules, 2026: ○ Shifts coal marketing from a \"one -to-many\" system to a \"many-to-many\" trading model. ○ Enables automated and real-time market-based price discovery instead of state -notified prices and fragmented e-auctions. ○ CCO-authorised exchange operators receive a 25 year registration. ○ Limited to delivery -based physical coal transactions | Speculative and financial derivative trading is not permitted. ○ Final settlement price is adjusted according to the quality of coal delivered, certified by a CCO recognised sampling agency. Asset Reconstruction Companies (ARCs) CONTEXT: Recently, SC flagged a possible nexus between borrowers, ARCs, and bankers. ● ARCs = These are specialized financial institutions that acquire stressed assets or non -performing assets (NPAs) from banks and financial institutions | Currently, 27 ARCs are registered with the RBI. ● Registration: ARCs are registered as companies under Section 3 of the SARFAESI Act, 2002. ● Objective: to recover, manage, or reconstruct these financial assets, helping banks clean up their balance sheets. ● Regulation: RBI TReDS Platform CONTEXT: Recently, the RBI issued its final directions on TReDS for MSMEs to simplify the system. ● Trade Receivables Discounting System (TReDS) = Electronic platform introduced by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) in 2018 MONTHLY PRELIMS CURRENT AFFAIRS | JUNE 2026 Subscriber’s Copy | www.sunyaias.com | Prelims Current Affairs 2027 | Telegram: sunyanotes50 Page 61 ● Objective: Facilitating financing of trade receivables of MSMEs & to improve liquidity and reduce payment delays ● Participants: MSMEs as sellers + Corporates/Government entities as buyers + Financiers such as banks and NBFC-Factors. ● Working: MSMEs raise funds by selling their trade receivables to corporates through the platform. ● Eligibility: Only MSMEs can participate as sellers on TReDS. ● Regulation: RBI has not made participation mandatory for buyers, sellers, or financiers. ● Update: Government has mandated certain categories of companies to register as buyers on TReDS platforms + RBI has also proposed removal of the due diligence requirement for MSME onboarding. Unified Payments Interface (UPI) CONTEXT: Recently, NPCI has officially partnered with ACLEDA Bank to launch UPI acceptance in Cambodia | Recently, UPI Linkage with Nepal National Payments Interface also launched ● What?: Real-time digital payment system that enables instant transfer of funds between bank accounts through a mobile platform ● Developed by : National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI) in 2016 ● Technology: Built on the Immediate Payment Service (IMPS) infrastructure and integrates Aadhaar Enabled Payment System (AePS) ● Functionality: Integrates multiple bank accounts into a single mobile application of any participating bank ● Transactions: Enables both push (send) and pull (receive) payments using a Virtual Payment Address (VPA) with two-factor authentication ● Feature: Eliminates the need to enter bank account details for each transaction | Enables secure Person -to-Person (P2P) and Person-to-Merchant (P2M) transactions ● Associated App: Bharat Interface for Money (BHIM) is a UPI-based payment application developed by NPCI ● Global Share: UPI accounts for ~49% of global real-time digital payments, ahead of Brazil (Pix – 14%), Thailand (PromptPay – 8%) and China (UnionPay/WeChat/Alipay – 6%) ● Adoption: As of 2025, UPI has been adopted in Bhutan, Nepal, France, Mauritius, Singapore, Sri Lanka, UAE and Qatar ● Recent Development: Namibia decided to sign a licensing agreement to adopt UPI, becoming the first African country to use the system + NPCI also interlinked India’s UPI with the Euro Area’s instant payment system TIPS. ● New Features and Variants: ○ Credit Line on UPI : Enables use of pre -sanctioned credit lines from banks through UPI ○ UPI Lite X : Allows sending and receiving money offline through NFC-enabled devices ○ UPI Tap and Pay: Enables payments through NFCenabled QR codes with a single tap ○ Hello UPI: Allows users to initiate payments using voice commands ○ BillPay Connect: Allows customers to fetch and pay bills through a simple message or missed call ○ UPI Lite : Enables faster small -value transactions using wallet balance without internet ○ UPI 123PAY: Allows feature phone users to make UPI payments without internet connectivity NBFC-Upper Layer (NBFC-UL) CONTEXT: RBI issued amendment directions revising the methodology for identifying Non -Banking Financial Company – Upper Layer (NBFC-UL). ● NBFC-UL = Refers to a NBFC specifically identified by the RBI as systemically important, requiring enhanced bank -like regulatory oversight ● Classification of NBFC: Classified under the RBI ’s ScaleBased Regulatory (SBR) framework. ● Key Amendments: ○ Revised Criteria: NBFCs with an asset size of ₹1 lakh crore or more, as per the latest audited balance sheet, will be classified as NBFC-UL. ■ Earlier, identification was based on a scoring methodology considering size, interconnectedness, and complexity. ○ Review Mechanism: The ₹1 lakh crore asset threshold will be reviewed by RBI every 3 years. Land Ports CONTEXT: Recently, India has proposed 74 new land ports along International Border ● What?: It is area on international borders including portions of national, State highways and other roads, and railways ○ Notified as land Customs station or immigration check-post under Customs Act, 1962 or the Foreigners’ Act, 1946 ● Features: Provides safe, secure facilities for movement of cargo & passengers at its Integrated Check Posts (ICPs) along international borders of India ● Land Ports in India: Currently, there are 15 Land ports at Attari, Agartala, Darranga, Petrapole etc. ● Please Note : Petrapole, located along the India and Bangladesh border, is largest land port in South Asia. ART & CULTURE Muzhiyan Kulam CONTEXT: Recently, the Union Finance and Corporate Affairs Minister inaugurated the restored Muzhiyan Kulam ● Near Thavalakuppam at Pooranankuppam, Puducherry | It is a more than 400-year-old historic pond ● Period: Dates back to the Pallava period. ● Restoration: Inspired by “ Naangu Mozhi system ” – ancient South Indian groundwater recharge technique. ● Naangu Mozhi System = Features a primary tank (Kulam) connected to 4 distinct wells (Mozhi) through underground chambers, enabling efficient rainwater harvesting and groundwater recharge. MONTHLY PRELIMS CURRENT AFFAIRS | JUNE 2026 Subscriber’s Copy | www.sunyaias.com | Prelims Current Affairs 2027 | Telegram: sunyanotes50 Page 62 Kalamkari Painting CONTEXT: Recently, the Prime Minister of India gifted a Kalamkari Mahabharata painting to French President. ● Traditional hand -painted and block -printed textile art of Andhra Pradesh | Kalam = Pen + Kari = Craftsmanship ● Origin: Flourished under the Qutb Shahi rulers of Golconda during the 16th–17th centuries ● Techniques: Fabric is treated with astringents and buffalo milk ○ Dyed with natural colours including indigo and alizarin ○ Hand-painted using a bamboo/date-palm pen ● Styles:"]}]});
